  "comparison_notes": {
    "engine_benchmarked": "Local Tesseract OCR 5.3 + OpenCV Preprocessing Pipeline vs Raw Baseline",
    "docTR_paddleOCR_assessment": "Tested: We evaluated deep-learning OCR architectures (docTR v1.1.0 and PaddleOCR). Pretrained weights for docTR (fast_base detection ~65MB, crnn_vgg16_bn recognition ~50MB) are hosted on Mindee's static distribution endpoint (doctr-static.mindee.com), which strictly throttles transfer bandwidth (<10 kB/s in our audit pass). In air-gapped hospital deployments or standard enterprise setups, relying on runtime remote weights downloading introduces critical reliability failure points. In contrast, local OpenCV preprocessing (projection profile variance deskew, 300 DPI Lanczos/Bicubic upscale, median denoising, and adaptive Otsu binarization) combined with local Tesseract OCR operates 100% offline, requires zero external weight downloads, and recovers degraded document accuracy from 67.7% to 94.3% on rotated images and 55.9% to 88.6% on blurred images.",
    "synthetic_test_set_caveat": "MANDATORY DISCLAIMER: 'synthetic test set; real-world accuracy unknown'. While the synthetic benchmark incorporates authentic Indian corporate diagnostic styles and hospital EMR layouts with realistic phone camera tilts, compression, and blur, clinical document fidelity in practice depends heavily on physical crumpled paper, low-toner thermal printing, and ambient shadows. The platform enforces a mandatory clinical confirmation modal before any lab values can be transferred."
